  "title": "Consistency and Predictability",
  "content": "Children feel safest when they know what to expect. Predictability reduces anxiety and helps children regulate their emotions. Consistency does not mean perfection; it means responding in similar ways to similar situations. When parents are consistent, children learn boundaries, routines, and emotional stability.\n\nThis lesson explores how inconsistency—often caused by stress, fatigue, or emotional overwhelm—can confuse children and lead to challenging behaviour. We also examine how predictable routines support brain development and reduce conflict.\n\nParents learn practical strategies for becoming more consistent, even during difficult days.",
  "activities": [
    "Choose one boundary you will keep consistent for the next week. Write down how you will respond each time.",
    "Create a simple morning or bedtime routine and follow it for three days."
  ],
  "reflectionQuestions": [
    "Where in your parenting do you feel most consistent?",
    "What situations make consistency difficult for you?",
    "How does your child respond when routines are predictable?"
  ]
